{
  "dom_id": "step-q6452",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Evaluate ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}\\frac{3n+2}{6}.$",
  "worked_solution": "The sum takes integer values of $n$ from $1$ to $4.$ So, computing the terms for $n=1,2,3,4$ and then adding them, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}\\frac{3n+2}{6} & =(\\frac{3(1)+2}{6})+(\\frac{3(2)+2}{6})+(\\frac{3(3)+2}{6})+(\\frac{3(4)+2}{6}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{3+2}{6})+(\\frac{6+2}{6})+(\\frac{9+2}{6})+(\\frac{12+2}{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}+\\frac{8}{6}+\\frac{11}{6}+\\frac{14}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5+8+11+14}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{38}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{2}\\cdot 19}{\\cancel{2}\\cdot 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
